{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"7489dbd4f720891a","name":"card_unit_universal_carrier","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/OceaniaStorm/units/card_unit_universal_carrier","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"UNIVERSAL CARRIER","text":"Your PLANS cost 1 less. When you play a PLAN, add a BLAST to your hand.","flavorText":"The Universal Carrier was one of the most common British armoured vehicles of WWII, with around 113,000 units built. It was developed from earlier Bren Gun and Scout Carriers, but the Universal design introduced in 1940 gave the British Army a single flexible tracked vehicle suited for many roles. It was small, open-topped, lightly armoured, and powered by a Ford V8 engine. Its usual crew included three or four men, with space for weapons, ammunition, stores, or passengers. The carrier was not meant to be a tank. Its value came from mobility and practicality. British and Commonwealth units used it to move Bren guns, Boys anti-tank rifles, PIATs, mortars, radios, and observation teams. In infantry battalions, carrier platoons gave commanders a fast way to shift support weapons across the battlefield. Reconnaissance units also used carriers beside scout cars. The vehicle appeared in nearly every theatre, from the Western Desert to Burma and Northwest Europe. Many variants were built, including mortar carriers, artillery observation vehicles, and the Wasp flamethrower version. Its simple design made it easy to adapt, which explains why it served long after WWII.","faction":"Britain","type":"tank","rarity":"Unique","cost":1,"operationCost":1,"attack":1,"defense":2},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_universal_carrier","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_universal_carrier_title","SourceString":"UNIVERSAL CARRIER","LocalizedString":"UNIVERSAL CARRIER"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::tank","attack":1,"defense":2,"range":1,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":1,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_universal_carrier_text","SourceString":"Your PLANS cost 1 less. When you play a PLAN, add a BLAST to your hand.","LocalizedString":"Your PLANS cost 1 less. When you play a PLAN, add a BLAST to your hand."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_universal_carrier_history","SourceString":"The Universal Carrier was one of the most common British armoured vehicles of WWII, with around 113,000 units built. It was developed from earlier Bren Gun and Scout Carriers, but the Universal design introduced in 1940 gave the British Army a single flexible tracked vehicle suited for many roles. It was small, open-topped, lightly armoured, and powered by a Ford V8 engine. Its usual crew included three or four men, with space for weapons, ammunition, stores, or passengers. The carrier was not meant to be a tank. Its value came from mobility and practicality. British and Commonwealth units used it to move Bren guns, Boys anti-tank rifles, PIATs, mortars, radios, and observation teams. In infantry battalions, carrier platoons gave commanders a fast way to shift support weapons across the battlefield. Reconnaissance units also used carriers beside scout cars. The vehicle appeared in nearly every theatre, from the Western Desert to Burma and Northwest Europe. Many variants were built, including mortar carriers, artillery observation vehicles, and the Wasp flamethrower version. Its simple design made it easy to adapt, which explains why it served long after WWII.","LocalizedString":"The Universal Carrier was one of the most common British armoured vehicles of WWII, with around 113,000 units built. It was developed from earlier Bren Gun and Scout Carriers, but the Universal design introduced in 1940 gave the British Army a single flexible tracked vehicle suited for many roles. It was small, open-topped, lightly armoured, and powered by a Ford V8 engine. Its usual crew included three or four men, with space for weapons, ammunition, stores, or passengers. The carrier was not meant to be a tank. Its value came from mobility and practicality. British and Commonwealth units used it to move Bren guns, Boys anti-tank rifles, PIATs, mortars, radios, and observation teams. In infantry battalions, carrier platoons gave commanders a fast way to shift support weapons across the battlefield. Reconnaissance units also used carriers beside scout cars. The vehicle appeared in nearly every theatre, from the Western Desert to Burma and Northwest Europe. Many variants were built, including mortar carriers, artillery observation vehicles, and the Wasp flamethrower version. Its simple design made it easy to adapt, which explains why it served long after WWII."},"operationCost":1,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::OceaniaStorm","EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardDrawnFromDeck","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardReset","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardSpawnedInHand","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardPlayedFromHand"],"hasSmokescreen":true,"spawnCardName":"card_event_blast;card_event_plan","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'Armored_Car_Move_New'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Units/Armored_Car_Move_New.0"},"soundMoveToFrontline":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'Armored_Car_Move_New'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Units/Armored_Car_Move_New.0"},"soundAttack":{"ObjectName":"SoundCue'AU_Tank_cannon_medium_fire_Cue'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Projectiles_and_Impacts/Tanks/AU_Tank_cannon_medium_fire_Cue.0"},"soundBlowUp":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'AU_ArmorExplosion_01'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Explosions/AU_ArmorExplosion_01.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/UNIVERSAL_CARRIER.UNIVERSAL_CARRIER","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":392.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_universal_carrier_photo","SourceString":"